{
  "name": "Home Seller Roof Inspections: How to Prepare Your Home",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1: Gather Your Roof’s Records",
      "text": "Before the inspector arrives, collect any previous repair receipts, maintenance invoices, or warranty information related to your roof. This helps them understand its history and differentiates normal wear from any recent work. Providing these details upfront can save time and clarify questions about past repairs or upgrades.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 2: Ensure Clear Access to the Roof and Attic",
      "text": "Make sure the inspector can safely and easily access all parts of your roof and, importantly, your attic. Clear away any debris from around the house, move anything that might obstruct ladders, and ensure any attic access points aren’t blocked. A clear path means a thorough inspection without delays.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 3: Clear Debris from Gutters and Roof Surface",
      "text": "Take a moment to clear any leaves, branches, or other debris from your gutters and the roof surface, if safely possible. This helps the inspector clearly see the roof’s condition, flashing, and drainage systems, making it easier to spot any underlying issues. A clean roof gives a clearer view.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 4: Point Out Any Known Issues or Repairs",
      "text": "Be upfront about any leaks you’ve experienced, repairs you’ve had done, or areas of concern. This transparency builds trust and helps the inspector focus on areas that might need extra attention. It’s always better to disclose information than to have it discovered later.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 5: Review Lender/Insurer Requirements",
      "text": "If you're aware of any specific certification formats, remaining-life statements, or other documentation required by your buyer’s lender or insurance, have that information ready. This helps ensure the inspector’s report meets all necessary criteria, preventing delays in the closing process. What to Do Next Once you’ve prepped your home, schedule a professional home seller roof inspection with Discounted Roofing Co. We'll ensure your roof is assessed thoroughly, providing you with the information you need to confidently move forward with your home sale.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 5
    }
  ],
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "speakable": {
    "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
    "cssSelector": [
      "h1",
      ".howto-step",
      "[itemprop=\"step\"]"
    ]
  },
